Lymphogranuloma venereum (Chlamydia)
Infectious Disease & Sexual Health
Painless transient genital ulcer, Tender unilateral inguinal buboes, Positive 'groove sign', Severe proctocolitis/tenesmus

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IDS-026
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of severe rectal pain, tenesmus, and a rapidly enlarging, highly tender groin mass requiring urgent assessment for an evolving abscess or complicated localized infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing exquisite, progressively worsening rectal pain, inability to defecate due to pain, and a highly tender fluctuant groin mass. Urgent physical assessment is required to evaluate for an impending bubo rupture, rule out an acute surgical perianal abscess, and initiate targeted systemic antibiotic therapy prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I have this agonizing swelling in my groin, and it hurts so much to pass a bowel movement that I feel like passing out."
History of Present Illness: Illness commenced approximately 3 weeks ago with a small, entirely painless, and largely unnoticed transient genital ulcer that spontaneously resolved. Over the past 7 days, the patient developed unilateral, severely tender, and significantly enlarged inguinal lymphadenopathy. Currently, the patient is experiencing severe proctocolitis presenting as painful tenesmus, rectal discharge, and severe perianal discomfort. Systemic symptoms including high fever, severe chills, and profound generalized myalgia have developed over the last 48 hours. Patient identifies as a man who has sex with men (MSM) and reports a new sexual partner 4 weeks ago.
Functional Status: Ambulatory with a severe limp, distressed when sitting due to rectal pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high level of acute pain and distress regarding the rectal discharge.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 98 | Temp: 38.4 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Visibly uncomfortable, shifting weight constantly, flushed and diaphoretic.
Physical Exam: Inguinal exam reveals a highly tender, fluctuant, 4cm unilateral bubo. A classic 'groove sign' is present, showing distinct separation of enlarged inguinal and femoral nodes by the tough Poupart's ligament. Digital rectal exam (performed with consent) is deferred due to extreme tenderness, but perianal erythema and mucopurulent discharge are visible. Clinical presentation aligns with the diagnostic tetrad: Painless transient genital ulcer (by history), Tender unilateral inguinal buboes, Positive 'groove sign', and Severe proctocolitis/tenesmus.
Environmental Observations: Clean apartment, easy access to hygiene facilities.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Taking over-the-counter paracetamol with minimal relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Lymphogranuloma venereum (Chlamydia trachomatis L1-L3) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Perianal Abscess - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of the massive, distinct inguinal bubo with the classic groove sign and the preceding history of a genital ulcer.

Syphilis (Primary/Secondary) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The extreme tenderness and fluctuance of the lymphadenopathy, whereas syphilitic nodes are classically painless and rubbery.

Chancroid - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The initial ulcer in LGV is transient and painless, whereas the chancroid ulcer is typically exquisitely painful and persistent.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with severe, complicated proctocolitis and a highly tender, fluctuant inguinal bubo, classic for the secondary stage of Lymphogranuloma venereum. The intense rectal pain causing voluntary constipation (tenesmus) and the high risk of spontaneous bubo rupture resulting in a chronic draining sinus tract necessitated an urgent, direct clinical evaluation. Delaying this assessment and the initiation of a prolonged course of targeted antibiotics until the next in-hours period carried a significant risk of fistulization or irreversible lymphatic stricture formation. This intervention strictly satisfies the criteria for an urgent after-hours review (MBS 585-600).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribe oral Doxycycline 100mg twice daily for a prolonged 21-day course. Request highly sensitive nucleic acid amplification testing (NAAT) via a direct rectal swab and first-catch urine first thing tomorrow morning. Advise against any needle aspiration of the bubo outside of a controlled specialist setting due to sinus tract risk.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking the Doxycycline immediately after a full meal and remaining upright for 30 minutes, the patient will completely avoid severe drug-induced esophagitis and nausea; experiencing a side-effect-free dose will reinforce strict adherence to the critical 21-day antibiotic regimen.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a specialist sexual health clinic for comprehensive STI screening.
DME/Safety (Optional): Sitz bath for rectal pain relief.
Follow-up: Urgent GP or sexual health clinic review within 48-72 hours to assess bubo fluctuance and review swab results.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the groin swelling suddenly bursts, if the rectal pain becomes completely agonizing and unrelenting, or if a high fever exceeding 39.5C develops accompanied by severe lethargy.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending specific NAAT swab confirmation; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The rectal pain and discharge will begin to improve within 3-5 days of starting the antibiotics, but the enlarged lymph node (bubo) may take weeks to slowly shrink and resolve completely.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

The large lump in your groin is an inflamed lymph node that is filled with fluid. Do not attempt to squeeze, pop, or drain it yourself under any circumstances, as this can create a permanent open wound tract that will not heal properly.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: This is an aggressive strain of Chlamydia that travels from the initial infection site into the deep lymph nodes and the lining of the rectum. It requires a much longer course of antibiotics (3 weeks) than standard chlamydia to completely eradicate the bacteria and prevent permanent lymphatic scarring.
Actionable Steps: Complete the entire 21-day course of Doxycycline, even when the pain stops. Take warm sitz baths to relieve the rectal pressure. Strictly abstain from any sexual contact until the entire treatment course is finished.

